## Why This Is Asked
Tests that simulate real user journeys in security-sensitive flows, like passwordless login, reveal how teams handle token security, expiry, and multi-device sessions under deterministic conditions.

## Key Concepts
- Passwordless authentication flows
- One-time tokens and expiry windows
- Session management across devices
- Test isolation with mocked email provider
- Deterministic timing for flaky-edge cases

## Code Example
```javascript
// Playwright sketch
test('passwordless login end-to-end', async ({ page, request }) => {
  await page.goto('/login');
  await page.fill('#email', 'user@example.com');
  await page.click('#send-link');
  // intercept and extract magic link from mocked email API
  const link = await getMagicLinkFromEmail('user@example.com');
  await page.goto(link);
  await expect(page).toHaveURL('/dashboard');
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test token revocation across multiple browsers and devices?
- How would you simulate clock skew or expiry boundary conditions in CI?

## Why This Is Asked
E2E with ML serving is common in production; tests must cover routing, feature delivery, latency, drift, and rollback.

## Key Concepts
- Canary vs Shadow routing
- Feature store integration and data drift monitoring
- Multi-region consistency and rollback triggers
- Observability and SLO validation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-test harness for ML-serving end-to-end
async function runETest(seed) {
  await seedFeatureStore(seed);
  const a = await requestModel('A', seed);
  const b = await requestModel('B', seed);
  assertClose(a.pred, b.pred, tol=0.01);
  assertLatency(b.latency, 400);
  // simulate drift and verify rollback path
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement drift thresholds and alerting?  
- How would you test rollback under partial failure (region loss)?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests that exercise keyboard accessibility demonstrate familiarity with a11y concepts and deterministic testing approaches for beginners.

## Key Concepts
- Keyboard navigation and focus management
- ARIA roles and attributes (role=dialog, aria-label, aria-live)
- Test determinism with seeded localStorage and deterministic delays

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example test outline in Cypress
describe('Keyboard accessibility for notes', () => {
  it('navigates via Tab, opens modal, saves, edits, deletes, and persists', () => {
    // seed
    window.localStorage.setItem('notes', JSON.stringify([
      { id: 1, title: 'Note 1', body: 'Body 1' },
      { id: 2, title: 'Note 2', body: 'Body 2' }
    ]));

    cy.visit('/');

    // Tab through list and verify focus
    cy.get('[data-testid="note-item"]').first().focus();
    cy.focused().should('have.attr', 'role', 'listitem');

    // Open create modal with keyboard
    cy.get('[data-testid="create-button"]').focus().type('{enter}');
    cy.get('[role="dialog"]').should('be.visible');

    // Create note
    cy.get('[data-testid="note-title"]').type('New Note');
    cy.get('[data-testid="note-body"]').type('New Body');
    cy.get('[data-testid="save-button"]').click();

    // Edit existing note
    cy.get('[data-testid="note-item"]').first().focus().type('{enter}');
    cy.get('[data-testid="note-title"]').clear().type('Edited Title');
    cy.get('[data-testid="save-button"]').click();

    // Delete note
    cy.get('[data-testid="note-item"]').first().focus();
    cy.get('[data-testid="delete-button"]').click();

    // Verify persistence after reload
    cy.reload();
    cy.window().then(win => {
      const notes = JSON.parse(win.localStorage.getItem('notes'));
      expect(notes).to.have.length(2);
    });
  });
});
```

## Why This Is Asked
This tests offline-first UX, real-time updates, and end-to-end data integrity in a geo-distributed delivery platform. It probes how you model network partitions, state synchronization, and failure handling across services.

## Key Concepts
- Offline-first: service workers, background sync, localStorage/IndexedDB
- End-to-end data integrity: idempotency, retries, reconciliation
- Real-time: WebSocket/MQTT driver streams
- determinism: mocks, seeds, time control

## Code Example
```javascript
// Playwright sketch to toggle offline and submit
const context = await browser.newContext({ viewport: { width: 360, height: 800 } });
const page = await context.newPage();
await page.goto('https://example.app');
await context.setOffline(true);
await page.click('#add-to-cart');
await context.setOffline(false);
await page.click('#checkout-button');
await page.waitForSelector('#order-confirmation');
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate exact ledger reconciliation across regional databases?
- How would you model flaky network conditions to ensure deterministic tests?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes proficiency with real-time systems in E2E tests, including multi-user concurrency, latency handling, offline scenarios, and data isolation. It reveals how you design deterministic, reproducible tests for complex client-server interactions.

## Key Concepts
- Real-time collaboration testing
- CRDT/OT reconciliation validation
- Multiple browser contexts orchestration
- Latency injection and offline/reconnect paths
- Data isolation and deterministic ordering
- Test data management and cleanup

## Code Example
```javascript
// Playwright scaffold (pseudo)
const docId = `test_${Date.now()}`
const context1 = await browser.newContext()
const page1 = await context1.newPage()
const context2 = await browser.newContext()
const page2 = await context2.newPage()

// Setup: both users open same document
await page1.goto(`/editor/${docId}`)
await page2.goto(`/editor/${docId}`)

// Concurrent edits with latency injection
await page1.route('**/socket.io/**', route => {
  setTimeout(() => route.continue(), 100) // Inject 100ms latency
})

await page1.type('[contenteditable]', 'User 1 text')
await page2.type('[contenteditable]', 'User 2 text')

// Verify real-time updates and CRDT reconciliation
const finalContent1 = await page1.textContent('[contenteditable]')
const finalContent2 = await page2.textContent('[contenteditable]')
expect(finalContent1).toBe(finalContent2) // Deterministic final state
```

